Extracting random sha-512 passwd for root
#mkpasswd -m sha-512 root
root(password name)
root:$6$GhA9S9oVP4IyMMFW$.ifjR8CYe3I/9PKAOPtU6tX5IQ/0YO.b0HhpWuhAcoAw7h11hdzHJ75RN6vEc2adGSxylyrHXmVOFYPr9HEFr/:15045:0:99999:7:: (root sha-512)

root:!:15045:0:99999:7:: (original file)
root:$6$GhA9S9oVP4IyMMFW$.ifjR8CYe3I/9PKAOPtU6tX5IQ/0YO.b0HhpWuhAcoAw7h11hdzHJ75RN6vEc2adGSxylyrHXmVOFYPr9HEFr/:15045:0:99999:7:: (modified file)
root:x:0:0:root:/root:/usr/bin/zsh (x redirects the /etc/shadow)
When changing the su root > it takes the password from /etc/passwd first instead /etc/shadow.


If the password is not in /etc/passwd > it takes password from /etc/shadow.

Use ss command instead of netstat ( netstat needs perimssion)
#ss -tulnp

ss: This is the command for socket statistics. It is used to dump socket statistics.
-t: This option filters the output to show only TCP connections.
-u: This option filters the output to show only UDP connections.
-l: This option stands for "listening" and filters the output to display only listening sockets.
-n: This option prevents the translation of hostnames and port numbers to names, showing numerical values instead.
-p: This option displays the process that is associated with each socket

(ALL:ALL) means users (represented by "ALL") the permission to run any command on any host as any user with elevated privilege.
